## REASONING

**Goals.** "Study runoff generation in the Naches" decomposes into (1) a mechanistic goal — understand how runoff is *partitioned* (surface overland flow vs. subsurface drainage) and *timed* (rain- vs snowmelt-driven) across the basin's heterogeneity — and (2) an implicit validation goal, since the only observations available are USGS stream gauges.

**Drivers.** The brief names elevation and soil. The Naches has ~1625 m of relief (352–1977 m). Under the NLDAS-2 default forcing this relief *is* partially resolved: precip/temperature vary with the nearest 12 km cell, so the rain/snow line and snowmelt timing — the dominant control on runoff generation in a Cascades basin — will differ across an elevation transect. Sub-12 km terrain is not resolved, so any within-cell orographic detail is lost. Soil (SSURGO) is a first-class lever and controls the surface-runoff vs infiltration/drainage split.

**Feasibility — the key honesty point.** Runoff *generation* at a column (QOVER surface runoff, QDRAI subsurface drainage, snowmelt QSNOMELT) is directly simulated and answerable. But the only validation data are **USGS stream gauges**, and ELM here is single-column with **no lateral routing** — there is no native integrated streamflow to compare against a gauge. Gauge validation therefore requires an explicit runoff-aggregation/routing step that the stack does not have. I mark this partial and list routing under requires_capabilities. Because subsurface drainage and water table are targets, columns need spin-up / a WTD-informed warm start.

**User's columns.** The user pinned three points. One, (46.62, **-119.90**), lies ~0.6° east of the domain's eastern edge (bbox max lon -120.5) — outside the Naches HUC. I retain all three per the request but flag this; I treat it as a low-elevation eastern-margin point and recommend confirmation. The three points give a rough elevation spread but do not cleanly span low/mid/high bands or soil classes, so I add a light augmentation to complete an elevation × soil stratification.

